    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Eleusis',
            subtitle: 'Shedding',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Eleusis is a groundbreaking card game that functions more as a simulation of scientific discovery than a traditional trick-taking or shedding game. Invented by Robert Abbott in 1956, it is a game of inductive reasoning where the rules of play are not known at the start. One player, acting as 'God' or 'Nature', secretly writes down a mathematical or logical rule governing which cards may follow others. The remaining players must intuit this rule through trial and errorâ€”observing which of their plays are accepted as 'legal' or rejected as 'illegal'â€”effectively practicing the scientific method to empty their hands.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Eleusis was created by Robert Abbott, a mathematician and puzzle designer. It first gained international fame when it was featured in Martin Gardner's 'Mathematical Games' column in Scientific American (June 1959).",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "4 to 8 players. One player is designated as 'God' (The Dealer) for the round.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two standard 52-card decks mixed together (104 cards total).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two standard 52-card decks mixed together (104 cards total).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "God deals 12 cards to each player. One card is placed face-up in the center to start the 'Main Line'.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Eleusis Express: Streamlined version: 12 cards each, no Prophet; players may guess the rule aloud on a valid play. No-play incorrect call costs 1 penalty card; correct call replaces hand with one fewer card. Scoring: 12 minus cards in hand, plus 6 for correct guess, plus 3 for emptying hand.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'The New Eleusis: Version with Prophet: 14 cards dealt to each non-dealer; player may become Prophet after valid play to judge moves; white and black markers every tenth card; elimination after fourth marker.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Eleusis is a groundbreaking card game that functions more as a simulation of scientific discovery than a traditional trick-taking or shedding game. Invented by Robert Abbott in 1956, it is a game of inductive reasoning where the rules of play are not known at the start. One player, acting as 'God' or 'Nature', secretly writes down a mathematical or logical rule governing which cards may follow others. The remaining players must intuit this rule through trial and errorâ€”observing which of their plays are accepted as 'legal' or rejected as 'illegal'â€”effectively practicing the scientific method to empty their hands.",
